INT. LOGGING CAMP MESS HALL - ARBITRATION HEARING - DAY

Long pine tables pushed into a U. Axes on hooks. Snow outside the windows. A coffee urn steams in the corner.

PETRA ILLES, 40s, plaid jacket over blazer, stands at the head of the U. REGINALD TOLLIVER, 50s, three-piece suit, sits opposite her with his hands steepled. OTTOLINE FRYE, 60s, the foreman, sits in the witness chair, a rifle-green parka zipped to her chin.

PETRA
Ms. Frye. Who signed off on the timber survey?

OTTOLINE
I did.

PETRA
Did you walk the ridge?

OTTOLINE
Not myself.

PETRA
Then how?

REGINALD
Objection. Foundation.

PETRA
She's the foreman.

REGINALD
She's not a surveyor.

OTTOLINE
I know a tree when I see one.

PETRA
Did you see this one?

OTTOLINE
I saw the stump.

PETRA
And the stump said?

REGINALD
Objection!

The door bangs. A CLERK, 20s, snow on his hat, hurries in with a folder, ducking as though the rafters were low. ABIODUN SHOLA, per the badge on his coat. Everyone looks.

ABIODUN
Sorry. Sorry. Urgent filing.

PETRA
Mr. Shola, we're in session.

ABIODUN
It's about the ridge.

REGINALD
Whose side is this?

ABIODUN
Yours, I think. Or hers.

He slaps the folder on the table between them. Petra and Reginald reach for it.

PETRA
Give me that.

REGINALD ^
Give me that.

They stop. Both draw back. Ottoline takes the folder, opens it. Reads.

OTTOLINE
(slowly)
That ridge was never ours.

The mess hall goes quiet. Even the coffee urn seems to stop bubbling.
